INT. DECOMMISSIONED LIGHTHOUSE - "INNOVATION LANTERN" OPEN-PLAN FLOOR - MORNING

Salt has crept into every corner of the converted tower, and the beanbags have gone faintly green with it. Five desks spiral up the inner wall like the thread of a screw, each one lit by a bulb that flickers whenever the old beam mechanism groans overhead.

DELPHINE OKONKWO-REYES, fifties, a woman who laminates her own compliments, stands at a whiteboard that reads SYNERGY IS A VERB in three different colors of marker.

BARNABY "BARN" TULLY, thirties, a sales lead in a vest too clean for the sea air, sits cross-legged on a beanbag, tapping a stylus against his teeth.

YUKI HASEGAWA, late twenties, the only one with a real coffee, watches the whiteboard the way one watches a slow leak.

OSWALDO "OZZIE" PRIETO, sixties, the building's last maintenance man, oils a hinge nobody asked him to oil.

MAEVE KILLORAN, forties, finance, holds a printout against her chest like a shield.

DELPHINE
Team. Team! Gather. Today we pivot from silos to... lighthouses.

BARN
Del, we are in a lighthouse.

DELPHINE
Which is why it's authentic, Barn.

Yuki sets her cup down very gently.

YUKI
Delphine, the quarterly lease line is eleven percent of --

MAEVE
Forty. It's forty percent, Yuki, I told you it was forty --

OZZIE
Beam's been out since March, you know.

DELPHINE
Ozzie, bless you, we're not discussing the beam --

BARN
Maeve, hang on, if it's forty then the pivot deck is wrong --

MAEVE
The pivot deck is a hostage note, Barn!

YUKI
Ozzie, is the lower stairwell flooding or not?

OZZIE
Little bit.

DELPHINE
(brightly)
We call that "organic flow," Yuki.

Maeve slaps the printout onto the nearest desk. The stylus stops mid-tap.

MAEVE
Eleven months of "organic flow." Eleven months of kombucha on tap and not one invoice paid on time.

BARN
That's not on me, Maeve, I closed Halloran --

YUKI
You closed Halloran with a refund clause --

DELPHINE
Everybody, breathe. Ozzie, tell them it's fine.

Ozzie wipes his hands on a rag, slowly, and looks up the long spiral of desks to the dead lamp.

OZZIE
Del. Honest answer? The tower's got a lean.

Silence. Then the whiteboard slides an inch down the wall and the marker tray clatters to the floor.

DELPHINE
(very quietly)
Nobody leaves until we align.

Maeve sweeps the printout off the desk and rips it in two, then in four. Barn is on his feet. Yuki is already pulling on her coat.

MAEVE
Align this, Del.

She throws the pieces. They flutter down the stairwell like snow.
